WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.370
Lucy Morgan was an artist.

00:00:01.370 --> 00:00:05.506
She had spent a week painting in a small country
town and decided the next day she could move on.

00:00:05.506 --> 00:00:07.439
She would go to a village called Kingston.

00:00:07.439 --> 00:00:09.920
But that night, Lucy Morgan had a strange
dream.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:13.420
She dreamed that she was walking up a dark
carved staircase and entered a bedroom.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:17.770
It was an ordinary room except for two things;
the carpet was made up of large squares that

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:21.680
looked like trap doors, and each of the
windows was fastened with big nails that stuck

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:22.680
up out of the wood.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:25.340
In her dream, Lucy Morgan went to sleep in
this bedroom.

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:29.400
During the night, a woman with a pale face,
black eyes, and long black hair came into

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:30.400
the room...

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:34.790
She leaned over the bed and whispered "This
is an evil place, flee while you can."

00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:38.680
When the woman touched her arm to her hair
along, Lucy Morgan awakened from the dream with

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:41.840
a shriek - she lay awake the rest of the night
trembling.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:45.910
Despite the little information in her story
"The Dream", part of the Scary Stories series,

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:49.470
the pale woman or The Pale Lady, as she'd
become known, has haunted the thoughts and

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:52.469
dreams of horror fans for decades, but who
is she?

00:00:52.469 --> 00:00:53.469
Where did she come from?

00:00:53.469 --> 00:00:57.500
In fact, where do any urban legends, folklore
and sleepover stories come from?

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:01.980
To find out how a legend is born a monster
walks among us, stick around to the end of

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:02.649
this video.

00:01:02.649 --> 00:01:08.612
(metal music )

00:01:08.696 --> 00:01:10.256
Welcome to Horror History.

00:01:10.256 --> 00:01:15.470
Few series in children's literature have remained
as popular and as controversial as Alvin Schwartz's

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:16.799
Scary Stories To Tell In The Dark.

00:01:16.799 --> 00:01:20.970
Inspired by Folklore, reimagined to film
and still topping banned book list to this

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:25.360
day, The Scary Story Series has dominated
sleepovers for decades and while the three-book

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:30.150
series features a full collection of frightening
short form tales, The Pale Lady of The Dream

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:34.020
published in Scary Stories 3: More Tales
to Chill Your Bones remains one of the most

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:38.351
puzzling and captivating creatures introduced
between the bleeding ink pages Scary Stories

00:01:38.351 --> 00:01:39.000
series.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:43.640
As always in Horror History, I'll be analyzing
the timeline. But the timeline of The Pale

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:47.540
Lady from the Scary Stories series is as mysterious
and twisted as her appearance.

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:52.189
So today, we're digging six feet deeper into
her origin from a Victorian dream to a living

00:01:52.189 --> 00:01:53.189
urban legend.

00:01:53.189 --> 00:01:54.189
Is she a monster?

00:01:54.189 --> 00:01:55.240
Is she a spirit?

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:58.250
Is she right behind me?

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:01.267
I'll sort all this out on the other side of
the horror history screen.

00:02:01.267 --> 00:02:06.428
(spooky music)

00:02:06.649 --> 00:02:09.970
In the story, "The Dream", a young woman, Lucy
Morgan, receives a warning in her dream from

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:13.590
a woman with a pale face, black eyes, and
long black hair telling her to flee that evil

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:14.234
place.

00:02:14.234 --> 00:02:17.700
After a quick huddle with her landlady, Lucy
moves to a different neighboring city, Dorset.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:20.780
She rents a room that's shockingly similar
to the dark room from her dream right down

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:22.099
to the trap door carpet.

00:02:22.099 --> 00:02:25.780
Convincing herself it's all cool, she answers
a knock at the door only to have The Pale

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:26.920
Lady from her dream enter.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:29.230
Lucy understandably takes her things and flees.

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:33.200
To understand what's going on on that page
and a half of text, let's start at the beginning.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:37.267
The author, Alvin Schwartz, first published
Scary Stories to Tell in the Dark in 1981.

00:02:37.267 --> 00:02:41.030
Schwartz usually leaves detailed background
references and notes in the back of all three

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:45.344
Scary Stories books but for "The Dream", he
simply wrote "This dream is a puzzle", which

00:02:45.344 --> 00:02:47.069
is kind of odd.

00:02:47.069 --> 00:02:50.780
Schwartz, prior to Scary Stories was a journalist
and prone to heavy research, particularly

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:51.780
in folklore.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:55.590
The stories included in his spine tingling
series are adaptations of tales retold around

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:59.989
the globe - from children raised by wolves
in Utah to cursed horses from Swiss legend

00:02:59.989 --> 00:03:04.200
but while most of the adapted pieces in Schwartz's
books have a specific regional etymology,

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:07.576
The Dream is one of you adapted from a slightly
different source.

00:03:07.576 --> 00:03:10.670
Victorians were super in to the supernatural.

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:14.750
Everybody had a ghost story that they really
believed and a home's haunted ghost room was

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:15.517
no joke.

00:03:15.517 --> 00:03:19.810
There were classic hauntings of headless soldiers
roaming halls and knocks on doors you do not

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:20.810
want to answer.

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:24.900
Augustus Hare was a British author of many
books in the Victorian era, including a seven-volume

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:27.201
autobiography called The Story of My Life.

00:03:27.201 --> 00:03:29.178
Really getting creative with the title there,
Augie...

00:03:29.178 --> 00:03:33.650
The book recounts nights spent inside of ghost
rooms and portentous dreams which have inspired

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:38.549
many horror works his death in 1903, including
a few others in the Scary Stories series.

00:03:38.549 --> 00:03:42.600
One example is "Like Cat's Eyes" - the tale
of a wife caring for her ill husband who is

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:45.439
frightened by the arrival of a hearse drawn
by four horses.

00:03:45.439 --> 00:03:49.379
"The Window" is another example and Augustus
Hare's version has its own notoriety.

00:03:49.379 --> 00:03:53.670
"Margaret found herself staring through the
window at a shrunken face like that of a mummy.

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:56.170
It's yellow green eyes gleamed like a cat's
eyes.

00:03:56.170 --> 00:03:59.750
She wanted to scream but she was so frightened
that she could not make a sound.

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:03.530
The creature broke the window glass, unlocked
the window, and crawled inside.

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:07.670
It came across a room and her terror was so
great that she could not scream and it came

00:04:07.670 --> 00:04:12.170
up to the bed and twisted its long bony fingers
into her hair and it dragged her head over

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:15.834
the side of the bed and - it bit her violently
in the throat."

00:04:15.834 --> 00:04:19.989
However, "The Dream", where we first meet The
Pale Lady was not a direct retelling like

00:04:19.989 --> 00:04:22.360
"The Window" and "Like Cat's Eyes".

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:26.699
It's more of a reimagining of one of the many
ghost tales included in Augustus Hare's mid

00:04:26.699 --> 00:04:28.470
eighteen hundreds autobiography.

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:33.020
As the story goes, Mrs. Butler while living
in Ireland dreamt of the most enchanting house.

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:36.951
Years later, she and her husband moved back
to London where she began searching for a home.

00:04:36.951 --> 00:04:41.560
Upon visiting an unusually low priced manner,
Mrs. Butler was shocked to discover that the

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:43.860
house was her house from her dream.

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:47.630
After purchasing it, they inquired as to why
the price had been so low at which point,

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:50.440
the agent revealed that the house was believed
to have been haunted.

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:54.140
Very convenient of her to disclose that after
the sale had already closed but now, they

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:57.340
had realized that Mrs. Butler had been the
ghost all along.

00:04:57.340 --> 00:05:00.810
She was astral projecting in her sleep into
the house years ago.

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:04.360
It's an interesting concept but still pretty
far off the story that we're familiar with

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:06.110
from "The Dream" in Scary Stories.

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:10.419
There's no mention of a pale lady or a warning
or even a trap door carpet. But there is a

00:05:10.419 --> 00:05:14.920
woman dreaming of a house and moving cities
only to end up in that very real domicile.

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:18.380
And though the names of the cities are different,
Kingston and Dorset, mentioned in The Dream,

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:22.110
are located in Britain where Mrs. Butler moved
and found her literal dream house.

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:25.700
Given that the tale originated from the Victorian
era, I'm going to assume that our setting

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:30.110
is sometime in the mid to late 1800s but let's
be honest, when we're thinking about The Pale

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:34.310
Lady, we're not thinking about when, we're
thinking about that face, and the appearance

00:05:34.310 --> 00:05:37.267
of this mysterious woman has a bit of history
of its own.

00:05:39.752 --> 00:05:41.038
[Screams]

00:05:42.229 --> 00:05:45.153
(impact)

00:05:46.808 --> 00:05:50.638
(eerie music)

00:05:50.638 --> 00:05:54.346
Since Scary Stories To Tell In The
Dark was first published in 1981, the books

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:58.380
have been subject to banning and removal from
libraries due to their graphic content, but

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:02.660
any die-hard fan will tell you, it's not the
story that haunts you, it's the illustrations.

00:06:02.660 --> 00:06:03.710
Dark and moody...

00:06:03.710 --> 00:06:07.660
Seemingly rotting on the page, illustrator
Stephen Gammell's macabre drawings of decrepit

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:10.319
buildings and empty eye sockets are powerful.

00:06:10.319 --> 00:06:14.889
So powerful that when HarperCollins reprinted
the books in 2011 with Gammell's artwork replaced

00:06:14.889 --> 00:06:19.910
with more kid-friendly versions from Brett
Helquist, petitions to bring back the OG arts

00:06:19.910 --> 00:06:21.880
before the reprints even hit the shelves.

00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:26.140
Despite many chilling creatures living between
the pages of Scary Stories, The Pale Lady

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:30.069
has become the most well-remembered image
from the hair raising series, beloved by horror

00:06:30.069 --> 00:06:34.401
fans and probably burned into the memories
of innocent children browsing their local library.

00:06:34.401 --> 00:06:38.230
The actual words of "The Dream" give a very
basic description of her black eyes and hair

00:06:38.230 --> 00:06:41.630
but the accompanying illustration creates
a whole other dimension.

00:06:41.630 --> 00:06:45.229
Many of Gammell's illustrations featured in
Scary Stories are ghastly but The Pale Lady's

00:06:45.229 --> 00:06:49.280
expression has been described as benevolent
which is supported by her role in the story

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:51.370
of warning off Lucy from the room.

00:06:51.370 --> 00:06:55.190
Stephen Gammell has never spoken at length
about his work in Scary Stories but commented

00:06:55.190 --> 00:06:56.669
about illustrating work in general.

00:06:56.669 --> 00:07:00.440
"The first time I read a manuscript, I can
immediately tell whether I want to illustrate it.

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:04.070
I may not know how the illustrations will
look but I get a certain feeling for the text.

00:07:04.070 --> 00:07:07.830
I respond to the words and if I can respond
to a story, I can illustrate it."

00:07:07.830 --> 00:07:11.199
Meaning that the illustrated design of The
Pale Lady is entirely inspired by the written

00:07:11.199 --> 00:07:13.840
word of The Dream and not an outside source.

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:17.730
The original source from Augustus Hare doesn't
mention a pale lady or pale woman anywhere

00:07:17.730 --> 00:07:22.280
in the text, nor does Alvin Schwartz reimagining
and Scary Stories offer many specific descriptors

00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:25.300
of her character beyond a very simple physical
description.

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:28.890
The impression of The Pale Lady is entirely
based on our own interpretation.

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:32.310
It's no secret that most of the short stories
featured in Scary Stories To Tell In The Dark

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:36.280
are common urban legends such as The Hook
but The Dream truly embraces the spirit of

00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:40.750
an urban legend by coming to life more and
more with each retelling and in the 2019 film

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:44.740
adaptation, Scary Stories To Tell In The Dark,
The Pale Lady was written out of the illustration

00:07:44.740 --> 00:07:46.639
and into another reality.

00:07:48.922 --> 00:07:49.993
– What's that?

00:07:52.711 --> 00:07:54.475
– [Stella] I can't believe it.

00:07:54.475 --> 00:07:55.700
It's her book of scary stories.

00:07:55.700 --> 00:07:59.780
– [Zac] The screenplay as adapted by Dan and Kevin
Hageman from a story by producer Guillermo

00:07:59.780 --> 00:08:03.810
del Toro who is no stranger to Victorian horror
follows a group of misfit teens who open a

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:07.230
book that they shouldn't have opened and release
the creatures of Scary Stories To Tell In

00:08:07.230 --> 00:08:08.760
The Dark into the real world.

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:12.669
An ironically similar concept is the film
adaptation for its fellow children's horror

00:08:12.669 --> 00:08:14.470
literature juggernaut Goosebumps.

00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:18.220
To be fair to the Scary Stories film, it's
pretty gnarly, just as with the books, while

00:08:18.220 --> 00:08:22.030
the Goosebumps film generally stayed in its
lane of kid horror, Scary Stories To Tell

00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:26.810
In The Dark does not shy away from body horror,
light gore, and some Victorian haunt sentiments,

00:08:26.810 --> 00:08:28.637
as one would hope and expect.

00:08:28.637 --> 00:08:30.389
– Our friend is a little nosy.

00:08:30.389 --> 00:08:33.909
– In most cases where a book is adapted to the
screen, the book is considered canon and I

00:08:33.909 --> 00:08:38.070
stick to that when analyzing the timeline
but with The Pale Lady, given its nature and

00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:42.148
continuously evolving legend, we can consider
the film to be more of a continuation of her

00:08:42.148 --> 00:08:42.808
legend.

00:08:42.808 --> 00:08:46.730
As noted, we surmise the events of the dream
are happening in Britain most likely during

00:08:46.730 --> 00:08:50.339
the Victorian age in the mid to late 1800s while the events of the film occur

00:08:50.339 --> 00:08:53.510
in 1968, in the US State of Pennsylvania.

00:08:53.510 --> 00:08:57.230
So it could be said that The Pale Lady first
appeared in Britain then reappeared in the

00:08:57.230 --> 00:09:01.720
small town of Mill Valley, Pennsylvania in
1968 after being unleashed from the book.

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:05.560
I assume the book that the kids find in the
movie would be similar in content to the Scary

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:07.260
Story trilogy in our world.

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:11.051
Only in the film, the book was not written
by Alvin Schwartz but by Sarah Bellows, a

00:09:11.051 --> 00:09:14.820
young girl locked in a basement by her family
at an undisclosed time in the past.

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:19.500
– [Stella] Sarah Bellows had a secret, a daughter that
they never allowed to leave the house.

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:22.430
The myth is that there was some really off
about her.

00:09:22.430 --> 00:09:25.750
– In her imprisonment, she wrote scary stories
that she would recite to kids listening on

00:09:25.750 --> 00:09:26.750
the other side of her wall.

00:09:26.750 --> 00:09:34.840
– And though they never saw her, they could
hear her through the wall.

00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:39.310
Sarah told them stories… scary stories.

00:09:39.310 --> 00:09:42.519
– So of course, they take the book which begins
to write stories all by itself.

00:09:42.519 --> 00:09:46.100
I hope when I die, I leave behind a book that
just writes horror history scripts but I'll

00:09:46.100 --> 00:09:49.410
probably just get the same thing that the
heroes of Scary Stories To Tell In The Dark

00:09:49.410 --> 00:09:54.760
got - stories about a murderous scarecrow,
a corpse toe, a jangly man, an infected cheek,

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:57.520
and a pale woman - stories that come alive.

00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:01.660
Each story rather than being a stand-alone
tale is related to a specific teenager's fear

00:10:01.660 --> 00:10:04.630
or just something that annoys them including
The Pale Lady.

00:10:04.630 --> 00:10:09.140
In this case, Chuck, one of our four teenage
victims, I mean, heroes, recounts a recurring

00:10:09.140 --> 00:10:12.110
nightmare to his friends describing someone
who sounds very familiar.

00:10:12.110 --> 00:10:14.850
– I had this messed up dream again.

00:10:14.850 --> 00:10:22.220
I was trapped in the red room and there's
that fat pale lady keeps whispering 'This

00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:25.982
is the evil place, run away while you still
can.

00:10:25.982 --> 00:10:30.010
– Soon after, the kids take a field trip to
a hospital looking for answers, like one does

00:10:30.010 --> 00:10:30.988
in a horror movie.

00:10:30.988 --> 00:10:34.740
There, Chuck separates from the group, which
is never a good idea, and wanders off to another

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:35.860
building on the grounds.

00:10:35.860 --> 00:10:40.190
He finds himself in a series of empty hallways
as the hospital alarm goes off and the light

00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:46.935
turns red and she appears - pale face, black
eyes, wide thin smile - The Pale Lady.

00:10:46.935 --> 00:10:48.935
(siren)

00:10:49.496 --> 00:10:52.814
(impact)

00:10:54.479 --> 00:10:58.042
(eerie music)

00:10:58.133 --> 00:11:02.670
In this adaptation, The Pale Lady is considered
by fans to be the most faithful representation

00:11:02.670 --> 00:11:04.000
of a character from the book.

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:07.220
The main difference is the ability to see
her feet, bare and pillowy as the rest

00:11:07.220 --> 00:11:10.079
of her, and a nightgown that seems to melt
into her skin.

00:11:10.079 --> 00:11:13.240
You can't determine where she begins and the
fabric ends.

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:17.600
Despite Chuck's dream, The Pale Lady doesn't
speak beyond unearthly murmurs as she shuffles

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:19.170
slowly towards him.

00:11:19.170 --> 00:11:22.570
Appearing in every hallway before cornering
him in the center, The Pale Lady gives Chuck

00:11:22.570 --> 00:11:27.244
a deep hug pulling him into her body with
a satisfied smile and she disappears.

00:11:28.589 --> 00:11:35.740
– [Stella] Chuck and Auggie are still gone but I know
there is a way to bring them back and that

00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:40.801
the secret is in the book and we won't stop
until we find them.

00:11:40.801 --> 00:11:45.500
– Until the unpleasantness with Chuck, The Pale
Lady hadn't been considered an antagonist

00:11:45.500 --> 00:11:47.090
and she still might not be.

00:11:47.090 --> 00:11:50.870
She was only trying to warn Lucy about the
danger of the house in that short story and

00:11:50.870 --> 00:11:54.580
until we know where Chuck disappears to in
the movie, I can't definitively say that The

00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:55.760
Pale Lady is evil.

00:11:55.760 --> 00:11:59.660
Perhaps with the spirit of Sarah Bellows stirring
the pot, The Pale Lady was trying to save

00:11:59.660 --> 00:12:03.120
Chuck in her own way by taking him to a realm
where he'd be safe.

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:05.630
But Sarah Bellows' inclusion does complicate
things.

00:12:05.630 --> 00:12:09.560
Alvin Schwartz's work was openly based and
adapted from folklore and other tales but

00:12:09.560 --> 00:12:13.240
within the world of the film, the spirit of
Sarah Bellows is writing these stories in

00:12:13.240 --> 00:12:14.280
real time.

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:18.060
That would imply that the urban legend, "The
Dream", never existed in the movie universe

00:12:18.060 --> 00:12:22.430
unless of course, Sarah is writing new lore
for a character from an existing legend.

00:12:22.430 --> 00:12:26.610
– You don't read the book, the book reads you.

00:12:26.610 --> 00:12:30.420
– At the beginning of this video, I mentioned
that there were two other tales and Scary

00:12:30.420 --> 00:12:34.090
Stories inspired by the accounts included
in the autobiography of Augustus Hare, "The

00:12:34.090 --> 00:12:35.709
Window" and "Like Cat's Eyes".

00:12:35.709 --> 00:12:40.029
Both of these stories are almost direct lifts
from their source material with the same beats,

00:12:40.029 --> 00:12:42.720
same characters, and even some word-for-word
dialogue.

00:12:43.101 --> 00:12:44.373
– Stories hurt.

00:12:45.718 --> 00:12:47.410
Stories heal.

00:12:47.410 --> 00:12:52.320
– So in this case, we're not analyzing her timeline,
we're building it, making her more real each

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:56.449
time we do so, starting in the mid-1800s with
the story of a woman who allegedly dreamt

00:12:56.449 --> 00:12:58.160
of a home that was proven real.

00:12:58.160 --> 00:13:02.200
Her tale was preserved within the autobiography
of Augustus Hare in 1859.

00:13:02.200 --> 00:13:06.930
Then in 1991, Alvin Schwartz publishes Scary
Stories 3: More Tales to Chill Your Bones,

00:13:06.930 --> 00:13:09.940
including the short story, The Dream which
introduces a character who would become known

00:13:09.940 --> 00:13:11.079
as The Pale Lady.

00:13:11.079 --> 00:13:15.350
In 2019, the film adaptation, Scary Stories
To Tell In The Dark reintroduces The Pale

00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:20.500
Lady in the year 1968, first is a dream and
then as a walking physical creature to audiences

00:13:20.500 --> 00:13:21.920
new and old.

00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:23.214
– Stories hurt.

00:13:23.214 --> 00:13:24.690
Stories heal

00:13:24.690 --> 00:13:27.750
If we repeat them often enough, they become
real.

00:13:27.750 --> 00:13:29.220
They have that power.

00:13:31.952 --> 00:13:33.440
They make us who we are.

00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:34.470
– Who is The Pale Lady?

00:13:34.470 --> 00:13:38.639
An urban legend that grows more visceral with
each retelling of the story.

00:13:38.639 --> 00:13:40.790
Who knows when we'll meet next.

00:13:40.790 --> 00:13:44.160
Let me know if there are any other scary stories
characters you'd like to see covered here

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:45.370
on Horror History.

00:13:45.370 --> 00:13:48.339
Remember to subscribe to CZsWorld for new
horrors every week.

00:13:48.339 --> 00:13:52.019
Ring the death bell and select all notifications
and I'll see you in the next one.

00:13:52.019 --> 00:13:54.135
Assuming we both survive.

